{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Yes. Beyond the ffmpeg/TTS execution risks, these areas need improvement:\n\n1. **Mute-first LinkedIn playback**\nThe plan relies on VO, but many LinkedIn viewers watch muted. Add burned-in captions or minimal kinetic text. At minimum, the six narration lines should appear visually.\n\n2. **Message hierarchy**\nThe film is trying to say three things: “Ineed AI ships like a team,” “AI agents peer-review each other,” and “video-claw has a $0 mode.” The operating-model idea should stay dominant; the $0 demo should be framed as proof, not a detour.\n\n3. **Shot 5 continuity**\nHard-cutting to `free-mode-demo.mp4` full-frame may break the review-room illusion. Even a rough Phase 1 screen composite would better sell “the agent is presenting this in the room.”\n\n4. **End logline implementation**\nThe design says the film ends with “This is how Ineed AI ships,” but the production plan does not specify typography, placement, duration, or whether it is burned into shot 10.\n\n5. **Visual consistency**\nGenerated stills may drift in cast age, uniforms, emblem shapes, office geography, and lighting. The plan should include a continuity pass, not just “verify 16:9.”\n\n6. **Brand-emblem risk**\n“Homage” logos could look muddy or legally awkward. Better to design clean fictional tribe insignia inspired by model families, then reserve exact logos for Phase 2.\n\n7. **Narration texture**\nThe script is clear but flat. It could use one sharper founder line, for example: “Every task gets reviewed before it reaches my desk.”\n\n8. **Audio polish**\nNo loudness target, no room tone, no music timing, no VO ducking, no final loudnorm pass. That will make the cut feel unfinished even if the visuals work.\n\n9. **Delivery package**\nPlan should include thumbnail/poster frame, captions/SRT, final LinkedIn caption, and maybe a square or vertical teaser crop. 16:9 alone is fine, but not complete for social distribution."}}
